<div class="bhd-page__keypoints" aria-label="Bicarbonate at a glance">
  <div><strong>An extracellular buffer</strong><span>Bicarbonate is a major component of the open carbon dioxide/bicarbonate system that helps regulate acid-base balance.</span></div>
  <div><strong>A sodium source too</strong><span>Sodium bicarbonate dissociates to sodium and bicarbonate, so one ingredient contributes two ions to the prepared drink.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Dose changes the story</strong><span>The physiological role of bicarbonate does not mean a hydration-serving amount should be treated as an ergogenic bicarbonate-loading dose.</span></div>
</div>

<div class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
  <article class="bhd-page__prose">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Normal physiology</p>
    <h2>Bicarbonate is part of an open buffering system</h2>
    <p>Carbon dioxide produced by metabolism can combine with water to form carbonic acid, which is in equilibrium with hydrogen ions and bicarbonate. The lungs regulate carbon dioxide, while the kidneys regulate bicarbonate and hydrogen-ion handling over a longer time scale.</p>
    <p>This makes the carbon dioxide/bicarbonate system unusual and powerful: one side of the system can be influenced by ventilation, while the kidneys help regulate the nonvolatile component. Bicarbonate is therefore not a niche sports ingredient invented for beverages. It is part of ordinary human acid-base physiology.</p>
  </article>
  <aside class="bhd-page__callout">
    <span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Important distinction</span>
    <h3>Buffering physiology is not a licence to claim “alkalization” benefits from any bicarbonate-containing drink.</h3>
    <p>Systemic acid-base balance is tightly regulated. Product-specific effects depend on dose, timing, formulation, health status and evidence from the finished product.</p>
  </aside>
</div>

<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">The chemistry</p>
    <h2>CO<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O ⇌ H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> ⇌ HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> + H<sup>+</sup></h2>
    <p>Carbonic anhydrase accelerates the interconversion of carbon dioxide and carbonic acid in tissues including red blood cells and the kidney. At physiological pH, bicarbonate is abundant in extracellular fluid and is central to the Henderson-Hasselbalch relationship used to describe this buffer pair.</p>
    <p>The practical takeaway is simple: bicarbonate participates in a dynamic physiological system rather than acting as a one-way “acid neutralizer” throughout the body.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__split">
  <div class="bhd-page__split-media">
    <img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/5078599/pexels-photo-5078599.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1400" alt="Spoon holding a measured powdered ingredient" width="1400" height="950" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
  </div>
  <div class="bhd-page__split-copy">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">In the Best Hydrate formula</p>
    <h2>Why use sodium bicarbonate instead of only sodium chloride?</h2>
    <p>Sodium bicarbonate contributes <strong>sodium</strong>, which is central to extracellular fluid balance and intestinal cotransport, while also contributing <strong>bicarbonate</strong>, a physiologically familiar extracellular anion. That gives the formula a different ionic profile than using sodium chloride alone.</p>
    <p>It also changes the solution’s acid-base chemistry and interacts with citric acid in the finished drink. The choice is therefore both physiological and formulation-related.</p>
    <p>We do not infer from that choice that the product raises blood bicarbonate enough to reproduce the effects seen in high-dose sodium bicarbonate supplementation studies.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Three contexts that are easy to confuse</p>
  <h2>Same chemical name, different purpose and dose</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--3">
    <article><h3>Normal body bicarbonate</h3><p>Bicarbonate is continuously generated, transported, reclaimed and excreted as part of normal carbon dioxide transport and acid-base homeostasis.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Hydration-formula bicarbonate</h3><p>A beverage may include sodium bicarbonate as a source of sodium and bicarbonate and as part of the solution’s formulation chemistry.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Ergogenic bicarbonate loading</h3><p>Sports studies commonly evaluate much larger acute doses, often around 0.2–0.5 g/kg body mass, to alter extracellular buffering before high-intensity exercise. Those protocols are a different intervention.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Performance research</p>
  <h2>High-dose sodium bicarbonate can affect exercise performance, but side effects matter</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
    <article class="bhd-page__prose">
      <p>Research and sport-nutrition position statements support ergogenic effects of appropriately dosed sodium bicarbonate in several high-intensity exercise tasks. Typical studied acute doses are far larger than the amount someone would infer merely from seeing sodium bicarbonate on a hydration-product ingredient list.</p>
      <p>Gastrointestinal symptoms including bloating, nausea, abdominal discomfort, diarrhoea and vomiting are well documented with bicarbonate-loading protocols and vary by dose, timing, delivery method and individual tolerance.</p>
      <p>Best Hydrate should therefore not borrow “bicarbonate loading” performance claims unless the actual finished product, dose and intended use have been evaluated appropriately.</p>
    </article>
    <aside class="bhd-page__callout">
      <span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Do not conflate</span>
      <h3>Ingredient presence ≠ supplementation protocol.</h3>
      <p>A hydration formula containing sodium bicarbonate is not automatically a bicarbonate-loading supplement.</p>
    </aside>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Oral rehydration history</p>
    <h2>Bicarbonate and citrate have both appeared in oral rehydration formulations</h2>
    <p>Earlier oral rehydration salt formulations used sodium bicarbonate. Modern WHO reduced-osmolarity ORS uses trisodium citrate dihydrate, and WHO documentation notes that sodium bicarbonate can substitute in certain immediate-use manufacturing contexts but has poorer stability under tropical storage conditions.</p>
    <p>This historical connection helps explain why both bicarbonate and citrate appear in oral-hydration chemistry, but it does not make a consumer hydration powder equivalent to WHO therapeutic ORS.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__brand-context">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Best Hydrate approach</p>
    <h2>Use bicarbonate for what it actually contributes</h2>
    <p>In our current formula, sodium bicarbonate is one part of an integrated mixture with sodium chloride, potassium chloride, glucose, citric acid and lemon powder. We highlight its contribution to the ionic profile and its relationship to established acid-base physiology.</p>
    <p>We intentionally stop short of claiming treatment of acidosis, therapeutic ORS equivalence, or an ergogenic bicarbonate-loading effect from the hydration product without the corresponding product-specific evidence.</p>
  </div>
